One indicator of wealth is electricity consumption. Europe has slipped back to the level of per capita electricity generation seen in the 1960s. Between 2000 and 2025, per capita energy consumption in the UK, France and Germany fell by 44 per cent, 27 per cent and 31 per cent respectively. In Japan, the fall was 24 per cent, and in the US, 20 per cent. In Russia, consumption rose by 28 per cent; in Brazil, by 44 per cent; in Turkey, by 89 per cent; in Iran, by 91 per cent; and in India, by 120 per cent. The clear leader in terms of growth is China, where electricity consumption has risen by 255 per cent over a quarter of a century.

Per capita energy consumption is highest in the US and Russia. 

